for every horror movie drawn from a  screenwriter's imagination  there's an equal number that has been  adapted from works of literary fiction  and short stories in particular seem to  be particularly well suited for a big  screen debut  here are the most frightening horror  movies based on short stories  one of the most ferocious horror films  of the 1980s  hellraiser was adapted by author turned  director clive barker from his own 1986  novella  the hell-bound heart barker has said in  interviews that the reason he wrote and  directed hellraiser was to exert some  creative control over adaptations of his  work  having been displeased with the feature  film versions of rawhead rex  and what became of his own script for  underworld  as a result hellraiser closely follows  the plotline of hellbound heart with  only a handful of changes  for example in the novella kirsty is a  friend of the ill-fated rory whose  brother frank  accidentally summons the cenobites while  in the film  kirsty is the daughter of rory who is  renamed larry  the name of the diabolical puzzle box  that conjures up the cenobytes  is also slightly different in the  novella it's the lemarchel configuration  which barker dubs the lament  configuration in his script  and speaking of the cenobytes their s m  inspired body modifications are  for the most part almost identical to  their description in barker's story  right down to the massive pins in the  creature which while nameless in the  book and film would eventually become  known as pinhead  hp lovecraft's hugely popular 1927 short  story the color out of space  has been adapted to film several times  most notably  die monster die with boris karloff and  1987's  the curse the most recent and by many  accounts  best screen translation is 2019's color  out of space  directed by south african filmmaker  richard stanley  stanley's version sticks closely to  lovecraft's story which is about a  meteorite that crashes on a new england  farm  and unleashes bursts of unearthly color  that caused horrific mutations to the  farm's flora  fauna and family there are a few changes  here and there for example  stanley's script adds some dark humor  and a dash of modern angst about climate  to the story  most importantly though color out of  space nails one of lovecraft's core  concepts  that unimaginable monstrosities lurk  just beyond our realm of understanding  always waiting for the right opportunity  to unleash their horrors on the world  all three film versions of the thing owe  their existence to john w campbell's  novella  who goes there first published in 1938  in the magazine astounding science  fiction  the novella depicts the battle between a  research team in antarctica  and the shape-shifting occupant of a  nearby alien spacecraft  of the three film adaptations it's john  carpenter's  classic 1982 movie the thing  that most resembles the plot of  campbell's novella from which it borrows  character names  the iconic blood test sequence and the  alien's transformative abilities  notably however the story of the thing  didn't actually end with who goes there  in 2018 a box of campbell's manuscripts  were discovered which revealed that the  novella was merely the shortened version  of a longer novel  titled frozen hell curiously frozen hell  appears to be an earlier version of the  tale that would become  who goes there campbell had seemingly  hoped to write the story as a full novel  but when he failed to sell it he  re-edited it into a shorter form and  sold it to astounding science fiction  the new updated story was finally  published in 2019  and a year later blumhouse productions  announced that a film version of the  expanded story is in the works  why don't we just  wait here for a little while  [Music]  see what happens three stories by  british author daphne du maurier were  adapted into film by alfred hitchcock  the novel's rebecca and jamaica inn and  the 1952 short story the birds which hit  the silver screen in 1963.  du maurier was inspired after seeing a  farmer attacked by birds while plowing a  field  and retained that image for her story  which pits a cornish farmer against a  growing army of enraged birds  screenwriter evan hunter expanded upon  domori's story by moving the action to  the bay area  adding numerous characters and striking  a slightly more humorous tone for the  movie's early scenes  what both projects share however is the  lack of concrete  reason for the birds to actually revolt  du maurier's story suggests that the  birds are set off by changing weather  patterns  while hitchcock alluded in interviews  that the attack was nature's revenge on  men  neither is ever confirmed however which  only makes the phenomena  in both film and story all the more  chilling  one of the greatest screams in 50s  science fiction horror  comes in 1958's the fly in which a  brilliant scientist has his genes  crossed with a house flies  [Music]  author james clavell wrote the script  for kurt newman's film  but the idea itself came from the short  story of the same name  by french british writer george langlin  the story which appeared in the 1957  issue of playboy magazine  was adapted to film with very few  changes the primary differences included  the fact that langlen's story takes  place in france while newman's film is  set in canada  clavel also spared the protagonist's  wife played in the film by patricia  owens from the more macabre  fate suffered by the written version of  the character langolin's story was also  credited as the inspiration for  two so-so sequels return of the fly in  1959  and curse of the fly in 1965 as well as  for david cronenberg's 1986 remake  as it turned out however the latter  movie was only loosely based  on langolin's original story in fact  writer and co-director cronenberg  only really retained the concept of the  fly in the transporter device  in order to tell his terrifying and  instantly iconic  tale of body horror an oscar nominee for  best foreign language film in 1966  the 1965 japanese supernatural anthology  film qui-dan  draws inspiration from the work of  lofkario hearn hearn was a greek irish  writer who wrote extensively about  japanese myth in the late  19th and early 20th centuries and his  stories  introduced western readers to the vast  array of ghosts and creatures that  populated japanese folklore  four of those stories were adapted for  quite on which was directed with  stunning visual flair by art house  favorite masaki kobayashi  being over half a century old the scares  here are milder than what you might have  come to expect from jay horror movies  however both hearn and quiet on found  inspiration in a creature that will be  very familiar to fans of the genre  specifically the vengeful on ryu  featured in two of her  stories are similar to the revenge  obsessed spirits depicted in the ring  the one missed call series and countless  other japanese  ghost movies mario baba's 1963 anthology  film black sabbath attributes its three  tales of supernatural horror to stories  by three different authors  whose last names should be familiar to  avid readers of 19th century world  fiction  de mon paso chekhov and tolstoy  but things aren't quite what they seem  here the tolstoy credited with the  source material for the vertilock isn't  word peace author leo tolstoy  but his second cousin alexie tolstoy who  was better known for his plays  and it wasn't anton chekhov who penned  the inspiration for drop of water but  ivan chekhov  who isn't actually a real person at all  but something of a joke by bava and his  co-writers  as for guimo gitmopassal's involvement  in the telephone  some historians suggest that this is  again a fabrication  though bava historian tim lucas has  suggested that the episode bears a  passing resemblance to demopezzle's  story  the orla notably that latter story was  itself made into a movie  diary of a madman which starred vincent  price and was released  the same year as black sabbath no matter  who penned each story black sabbath is  one of bava's most  enjoyable and spine chilling works  thanks in no small part of course to the  legendary boris karloff  who both narrates the film and features  in the vertilock  richard matheson's name comes up  countless times in the history of horror  fantasy and science fiction in the 20th  century  he's linked to some of those genres most  influential and popular films and  television series  from the twilight zone and i am legend  to star trek and bid time return the  novel that became somewhere in time  matheson is also responsible in part for  helping to launch steven spielberg's  film career  with the 1971 tv movie duel  matheson adapted his own short story  published in playboy in 1971  for the abc movie of the week the script  about a motorist pursued by a faceless  and relentless trucker  caught the attention of spielberg who  earned huge ratings and  widespread critical praise for his  nail-biting mix of suspense  and motorized action in fact dual was so  popular that it was released to theaters  in international markets which in turn  helped to pave the way for spielberg's  motion picture career he later paid  tribute to the big break afforded by  duel  by inserting the truck's monster-like  roar into the finale of jaws  when the shark's body sinks into the  ocean  horror fans critics and filmmakers alike  have cited cult filmmaker nicholas  rogue's 1973 featured don't look now  as one of the best and most influential  supernatural thrillers of the 1970s  for the film alan scott and chris bryant  adapted the short story of the same name  written in 1971 by hitchcock favorite  daphne du maurier  don't look now stars donald sutherland  and julie christie as grieving parents  who come to believe that their late  daughter may be trying to warn them  about an impending threat  she's with you she's with you my dear  and she's laughing the script closely  follows the source materials storyline  including its shocking final revelation  and conclusion meanwhile rogue's unique  editing and color schemes have  influenced countless filmmakers over the  years  among the many filmmakers who have paid  homage to don't look now  is joel schumacher whose 1990 film  flatliners even features an overt  reference to the deadly figure in red  who features in rogue's film stuart  gordon's  re-animator features some of the most  gruesome scenes in  any horror film of the 1980s you might  be forgiven for thinking that  reanimator's more  outrageous moments were conceived by  gordon himself  but the source material hp lovecraft's  herbert west  reanimator is actually even gorier than  the movie  gordon's script streamlined lovecraft  story 2 which takes place over the  course of several years and even takes  its anti-heroes to the battlefields of  world war 1 in search of bodies to  reanimate  and the sheer nastiness of herbert west  is more than a match for gordon's  supercharged on-screen gore  west's reanimated subjects carry out  multiple killing sprees  and at one point even turn cannibal  lovecraft's story also features an  animated body without a head  which leaves a zombie army against the  west in the blood-soaked final chapter  if anything it seems like gordon  actually had to reel  in the violence for the movie version of  reanimator which is pretty remarkable  considering what did end up on screen  mgm's freaks has shocked and amazed  viewers since  its debut in 1932. the film directed by  todd browning  features actual sideshow performers in a  macabre gothic story of  love betrayal and revenge one that has  earned as much praise as it has  condemnation  numerous writers including uncredited  future oscar winner charles macarthur  worked on the script which was inspired  by todd robin's 1926 story  spurs but freaks isn't actually all that  similar to spurs anyone who reads the  actual story will find that very little  connects the two projects  beyond the carnival setting and the  ill-fated love triangle between three of  its performers  horror fans may also be disappointed to  find that the fate of the story's  so-called normal characters is somewhat  less disturbing than what happens to  trapeze artist cleopatra  and strongman hercules in browning's  film haven't seen the movie either  well let's just say things don't turn  out well  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one

